// SOS M3 unit 2.75: A MALFORMED WORD NAMES NOTHING AT EVERY GENERATION (sawos
// design 3 D-1, as amended by the Aug-29 user note).
//
//   SOS malformed: releasing a word whose index field is zero
//   SOS: process fault: bad handle process=0
//   SOS: process teardown handles=3 ... process=0
//
// **THE REGRESSION THIS EXISTS TO CATCH.** Before the word was split, "names
// nothing" was `word == NO_HANDLE` and that test was complete. With a split
// word it is not: `0x100` is a generation of 1 over an index field of ZERO, it
// is NOT equal to `NO_HANDLE`, and a lookup that kept the old whole-word test
// would take it as a real handle and index the table with it. The rule the
// kernel implements instead is that the INDEX FIELD carries a 1-BASED index, so
// zero is not an index — at any generation — and `lookup_handle` tests the field.
//
// THE PROBE IS VALID AT EVERY SPLIT, which matters because `HANDLE_INDEX_BITS`
// is configurable. At the default eight bits this word is index 0 at generation
// 1, refused because zero is not an index. At a WIDER index field it is index
// 256, refused because the table has nothing like that many entries. Either way
// it names nothing, and either way a kernel that let it through would be reading
// out of bounds or handing out slot 0 — so the case keeps checking the thing it
// was written for without knowing where the boundary sits.
//
// It is spelled at the C altitude for the reason every case in this family is:
// the typed layer has no way to build a handle from a word, which is the ruled
// guarantee, so the mistake only exists one floor down.
//
// ONE FAULT PER CASE: a fault ends the process.

import sos.{System, sos_handle_release}

static EXIT_FAILED: UInt = 1

/// A word a hostile or buggy process might write: generation bits set over an
/// index field of zero.
///
/// A DELIBERATE MAGIC NUMBER, which is the one place in this tree that is the
/// right thing to write. It is not derived from `HANDLE_INDEX_BITS` because the
/// point is that it is UNTRUSTED INPUT — a number chosen by the process, not by
/// the encoding — and the claim is that the kernel refuses it whatever the
/// encoding happens to be. See the header for why it is refused at every split.
static MALFORMED: UInt = 0x100

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS malformed: releasing a word whose index field is zero\n")

    // Does not return.
    let _ = sos_handle_release(MALFORMED)

    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS malformed: survived, which is the bug\n")
    let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
}
